# KEEP IT AT THE BOTTOM OF THE CHAT.
#
# Telegram has no sticky footer. A pinned message lives in a bar at the TOP, and
# the only way a post sits at the BOTTOM is to be the newest one. So when the
# figures actually change, this REPOSTS rather than edits: new photo, pinned,
# old one deleted. The card is then both the pinned message and the last thing
# in the channel, which is what a reader scrolling to the end sees.
#
# It reposts ONLY on a real change. The periodic "as of" refresh still edits in
# place, because moving the card to the bottom every few hours to say nothing
# new is how a banner teaches people to scroll past it.
KEEP_AT_BOTTOM = os.environ.get("BANNER_KEEP_AT_BOTTOM", "0") == "1"

# text | card
#
# TEXT IS THE DEFAULT, AND THE PHOTO CARD WAS THE WRONG SHAPE FOR THIS JOB.
#
# The one thing Telegram keeps permanently on screen in a channel is the PINNED
# MESSAGE BAR at the top. It is always there, however many messages arrive, and
# it shows a ONE-LINE PREVIEW of whatever is pinned. That bar is the widget.
#
# A 1200x630 photo previews in that bar as a thumbnail plus a clipped caption:
# bulky, and the number -- the only thing anyone wants from it -- is not on the
# first line. A short text message previews as exactly its first line, so
# putting the figure there makes the pin bar read as a live status strip:
#
#     PCoin listing: 10 / 1,000 purchases
#
# It also removes the need to repost. The pin bar does not scroll away, so
# chasing the bottom of the chat buys nothing and costs a deleted-and-reposted
# message every sale. Hence KEEP_AT_BOTTOM now defaults OFF; set it to 1 only if
# you deliberately want the card style chasing the newest position.
STYLE = os.environ.get("BANNER_STYLE", "text").strip().lower()
if STYLE not in ("text", "card"):
    STYLE = "text"

# The public announcement channel. Hardcoded so it can never be reached by a
